; os, on the JVM — the LIBRARY layer, not the target's.
;
; TOTALISATION, ON THE HOST WHERE FAILURE IS IN THE TYPES. See `lib/os/js.oro`
; for the algebra; the short form is that a fallible call is a partial map and a
; partial map is a total one into a coproduct, no host gives that coproduct, and
; so a cell declares the CALL that produces the pair and the DISCRIMINATOR that
; reads it.
;
; Java's checked exceptions make no difference to us and that is worth saying:
; `throws IOException` is a demand on the CALLER's syntax, and the caller here is
; a template, which catches. The type system that makes this host's failures
; loudest is the one that costs us the least. (`error` is `Exception` on this
; target and is declared in `targets/java/java.oro`, because how a host spells an
; error value is a fact about the target rather than about any package.)
;
; AND THE JVM CHARGES FOR A BYTE, WHICH THE OTHER TWO DO NOT.
;
; `Files.readAllBytes` gives a `byte[]`, and the JVM's byte is SIGNED, so 0..255
; does not fit it — the exact fact elemwidth-2026-08-27 found when a declared
; `(array (int 0 255))` came out `short[]` here and `[]byte` on Go. A file's
; bytes ARE 0..255; declaring them −128..127 would make `(src i)` return −1 for
; 0xFF and the same program answer differently on different hosts, which is what
; ADR 0009's rule forbids one level down. So this host WIDENS on the way in and
; NARROWS on the way out, one pass each, at the boundary and nowhere else.
;
; That is the parasite model pricing something honestly rather than hiding it:
; Go and Node read a file for free, the JVM pays a copy, and the cost is written
; in the cell that knows why.

(provides java os
  ; `%r0` and `%r1` are the DESTINATIONS. A `try`/`catch` is a statement and not
  ; an expression yielding a pair, so a host that signals failure out of band has
  ; to be given somewhere to put its two values — the second of the format's two
  ; multi-result shapes, and the one every throwing host needs. The emitter
  ; declares them and javac's definite-assignment check then enforces that every
  ; arm writes every one.
  ;
  ; `$oroB`/`$oroI`/`$oroE` are scoped to the `try` block and are spelled so they
  ; cannot collide with an emitted local: Java forbids shadowing one, and
  ; `javaMangle` never produces a name starting with `$oro`.
  (sig ReadFile ((path string)) (tuple (array (int 0 255)) error) (host expr
      "try { final byte[] $oroB = Files.readAllBytes(Path.of(%s)); %r0 = new short[$oroB.length]; for (int $oroI = 0; $oroI < $oroB.length; $oroI++) %r0[$oroI] = (short) ($oroB[$oroI] & 0xFF); %r1 = null; } catch (Exception $oroE) { %r0 = null; %r1 = $oroE; }"
      (import "java.nio.file.*")))

  (sig WriteFile ((path string) (data (array (int 0 255))) (perm int)) error (host expr
      "try { final short[] $oroS = %s; final byte[] $oroB = new byte[$oroS.length]; for (int $oroI = 0; $oroI < $oroS.length; $oroI++) $oroB[$oroI] = (byte) $oroS[$oroI]; Files.write(Path.of(%s), $oroB); %r0 = null; } catch (Exception $oroE) { %r0 = $oroE; }"
      (import "java.nio.file.*")))

  ; The discriminator.
  (sig err-nil ((e error)) bool pure (host expr "%s == null"))

  ; Bytes to text, ONCE, at the boundary.
  ;
  ; A LAMBDA APPLIED AT THE CALL SITE, and not because it reads well: `%s` is an
  ; arbitrary expression — `(text-of (format src))` is the real call — and every
  ; array-narrowing form Java has that is an EXPRESSION mentions its operand
  ; twice. Once bound to a parameter it is evaluated once. This is callbacks.md
  ; tier 1 exactly: the lambda never becomes a value in our residual, the host
  ; closes over it, and C2 inlines it.
  (sig text-of ((b (array (int 0 255)))) string
    pure (host expr
      "((java.util.function.Function<short[],String>) ($oroA) -> { final byte[] $oroB = new byte[$oroA.length]; for (int $oroI = 0; $oroI < $oroA.length; $oroI++) $oroB[$oroI] = (byte) $oroA[$oroI]; return new String($oroB, StandardCharsets.UTF_8); }).apply(%s)" (import "java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets")))

  ; THE COMMAND LINE IS THE ONE THING A JVM PROGRAM CANNOT ASK FOR. Go has
  ; `os.Args` and Node has `process.argv`; the JVM hands its arguments to `main`
  ; and offers no global, so this is the only declaration in the repository whose
  ; implementation needs the program's LAYOUT to cooperate — `oroArgs` is emitted
  ; by the Java program writer, and only when something reads it.
  (sig Args () (array string) (host expr "oroArgs"))
  (sig Getenv ((k string)) string pure (host expr "System.getenv().getOrDefault(%s, \"\")")))
